From: cnjn <unknown>
Subject: [PATCH] libfstools: use an overlay in RAM when the overlay volume cannot be mounted

mount_overlay() returned the mount error and mount_root() ignored it, so a
volume that JFFS2 refuses to mount (a block whose page ECC does not read back)
left the system on the read-only root file system: no host name, no network
configuration and no way to repair it over the network. The pivot below already
falls back to a RAM overlay; do the same for the mount itself.

Adapted from cnjn/immortalwrt package/system/fstools/patches/203 for the fstools
snapshot this tree pins (2aa16407fdedb3f1e1b915879f4d6772c5fe896c); his hunk
carries shared-volume context this tree does not have, so the context here is
regenerated from that snapshot.

diff --git a/libfstools/overlay.c b/libfstools/overlay.c
--- a/libfstools/overlay.c
+++ b/libfstools/overlay.c
@@ -453,8 +453,16 @@ int mount_overlay(struct volume *v)
 	}
 
 	err = overlay_mount_fs(v, overlay_mp);
-	if (err)
-		return err;
+	if (err) {
+		/*
+		 * Without an overlay the system would stay on the read-only root
+		 * file system: no host name, no network configuration and no way to
+		 * repair it over the network. Fall back to RAM, as below does when
+		 * the pivot fails, and say so.
+		 */
+		ULOG_ERR("cannot mount the overlay volume, using a temporary overlay in RAM: settings will not survive a reboot\n");
+		return ramoverlay();
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * Check for extroot config in overlay (rootfs_data) and if present then
